// Explanations distinguish public principles from the authored comparisons.
export const STUDY_GUIDES = [
  {id:'electrification-chain',domain:'power',view:'electrification',component:'study-collector',title:'Read the complete electrification chain',summary:'Changing current collection also changes the equipment before and after it. Follow both sides of the interface.',sections:[
    {heading:'Compare complete arrangements',text:'Start with the DC conductor comparison, then choose AC overhead. Watch the wayside rectifier and collector shoes disappear as the contact wire, pantograph, onboard transformer and line converter appear. This study uses a rotary drive in both cases so the supply comparison remains clear. It is separate from the stored propulsion and train-control choices used elsewhere in the atlas. Siemens’s public AC overview identifies the supply transformer and switchgear as parts of an AC traction supply; the model’s layout and equipment proportions are original.',sources:['E01','P01']},
    {heading:'Keep the transformer’s circuits separate',text:'The conventional chain in ABB’s Figure 2 is the reference for the AC vehicle. The pantograph feeds the onboard transformer’s primary circuit, with a return through wheels and running rails. The secondary supplies a line converter and DC link before the motor inverter. In this model amber, cyan and copper traces let you inspect those different relationships. They must not be read as one wire flowing straight through the transformer. Open the enclosure to distinguish the line-converter module from the DC-link capacitor bank, then follow the inverter’s mechanical destination at the axle.',sources:['E02','E03','P04']},
    {heading:'Ask what the comparison leaves open',text:'The selected DC example uses a separate return conductor; that choice is not universal for DC railways. The AC example is a conventional architecture, without the experimental transformer also discussed in the ABB article. Neither example supplies ratings, protection settings, section transitions, neutral sections, bonding arrangements or an electrical isolation method. Those questions need a defined railway and additional evidence. Use the study to locate where the interfaces change, then return to the original substation or vehicle model for its different scope.',sources:['E02','P01']},
  ],sources:['E01','E02','E03','P01','P04']},
  {id:'wheel-rail-response',domain:'vehicle',view:'adhesion',component:'adhesion-contact',title:'Separate wheel rotation from vehicle response',summary:'Demand, wheel torque, contact conditions and vehicle travel are connected, but they are not the same observation.',sections:[
    {heading:'Begin with the two motion cues',text:'The axle carries a rim marker while a separate marker follows its centre against a fixed reference. Animate rolling, spin and slide, or move the phase slider. The centre follows the same authored path so the wheel’s different rotation is easy to compare. These rates are illustrative, without mass, inertia, adhesion coefficients or a force calculation. The scene concerns a rotary axle drive and friction braking. It does not transfer those traction relationships to the LIM, whose thrust is developed through a reaction rail.',sources:['H01','P02']},
    {heading:'Inspect two different interventions',text:'Wheel-slide protection modulates brake effort; sanding introduces material at the wheel–rail interface. The protection comparison changes the actuator cue and wheel rotation. The sanding comparison instead exposes a reservoir, hose, nozzle and grains arriving ahead of the contact. Knorr-Bremse’s public equipment descriptions distinguish these mechanisms, and RSSB’s research discusses low adhesion and mitigation. The model does not adopt a supplier’s performance figures or implement a real controller. Sand appearing at the rail is not proof that a particular braking performance has been restored.',sources:['H01','H02','T08']},
    {heading:'Connect response with measurement and maintenance',text:'An axle sensor reports rotation. The independent travel cue represents the separate question of how the vehicle is moving; it is not a particular sensor installation. Use the connected concepts to examine localization and the depot wheelset. A discrepancy can prompt investigation, but this scene supplies no diagnostic threshold, wheel-damage prediction or maintenance acceptance criterion. A requested effort, an observed motion and an accepted condition need their own evidence.',sources:['T06','T07','H01']},
  ],sources:['H01','H02','T08','T06','T07','P02']},
  {id:'infrastructure-work',domain:'track',view:'infrastructure-care',component:'route-inspection',title:'From infrastructure observation to follow-up evidence',summary:'Track, ground, drainage and structures meet in one location. Work on one interface does not close every observation there.',sections:[
    {heading:'Read the support and water paths',text:'The section places track on an earthwork beside a culvert and a small bridge. The formation carries the track support relationship; the culvert and outfall provide a separate water path. Network Rail’s public explanations connect slope condition and drainage, while its inspection example shows why some spaces need more than a view from track level. The geometry here is fictional. It contains no calculated earth pressure, water capacity, structural load or failure threshold.',sources:['I01','I02','I03']},
    {heading:'Inspect, intervene, then check',text:'Inspection shows a probe at the culvert and a marker at a bridge bearing. Work in progress adds a cleaning hose and removes the visible drain obstruction. Work complete removes the tool while leaving an open follow-up entry. Check recorded adds a separate inspection result for the drainage location. These are chosen states, not a work method or a timed repair sequence. The fixed boundary remains in place throughout. Its appearance conveys a distinct work location, without demonstrating track protection or permission to enter.',sources:['I02','Q01']},
    {heading:'Preserve the other question',text:'The bearing observation remains visible after the drainage work and follow-up entry. It is a useful reminder to ask which asset and condition a piece of evidence covers. A repaired drain does not establish the condition of a nearby structure, and a recorded inspection does not automatically authorize the return of a route to service. Follow the record connection into Configuration & handover to examine the separate subject, result, issue and decision records.',sources:['I03','Q01']},
  ],sources:['I01','I02','I03','Q01']},
  {id:'service-dependencies',domain:'operations',view:'dependencies',component:'dependency-services',title:'Trace the dependencies behind a resilient service',summary:'An alternate data path helps only within the functions and dependencies that it actually covers.',sections:[
    {heading:'Name the function before discussing redundancy',text:'Two applications support fictional station information and power supervision. The communication paths are separate from their shared power bus, operator identity, configuration and recording. Select Path A lost: station information retains its alternate conduit, while power supervision loses its illustrated connection. Select Shared power lost: neither live service trace remains. The field equipment stays physically present in both comparisons. This is a dependency study, without an inference that equipment would automatically transfer between paths.',sources:['R01','R02']},
    {heading:'Distinguish access, live information and history',text:'Identity unavailable retains the field-indication path but removes the new operator-request connection. Recorder unavailable retains live interfaces while interrupting the record branch. These are selected functional boundaries of the fictional example, not universal behavior for sessions or railway control systems. ENISA’s railway report provides the context of asset groups and connecting conduits. The illustration provides no actual network address, access rule, software weakness or attack demonstration.',sources:['R01','R02']},
    {heading:'Restore evidence as well as a connection',text:'NIST’s published Revision 3 discusses recovery of OT state, data, configuration and programs. Here, Restored, unchecked reconnects the conduit while retaining a configuration mismatch and recording gap. Evidence checked adds separate checks without filling that gap. The comparison supplies no recovery time, synchronized replica or authorization to operate. Open the configuration study to see why a record must identify the actual equipment version. The reference is the published 2023 revision; the later revision listed as a draft at review is not treated as adopted guidance.',sources:['R01','Q01']},
  ],sources:['R01','R02','Q01']},
  {id:'passenger-journey',domain:'station',view:'accessible-journey',component:'access-entrance',title:'Follow access through the whole passenger journey',summary:'A usable route connects physical passage, understandable information, assistance and the boarding interface.',sections:[
    {heading:'Inspect the connected interfaces',text:'Begin at the street, pass the wide fare gate and follow the near lift to the platform. The tactile strip, display and help point are distinct equipment; their information links are not passenger paths. The ERA application guide connects obstacle-free routes with changes of level, identification and other station interfaces. It is EU guidance rather than a universal rulebook. This model specifies no compliant width, gradient, contrast, illumination or platform gap.',sources:['A01']},
    {heading:'Change the journey when a facility is unavailable',text:'Lift unavailable stops the highlighted route before the near lift and exposes a service message and assistance connection. The stairs remain, but the step-free route is not redirected onto them. Alternate lift selects a different approach through the fictional station and preserves the original outage marker. TransLink’s public guidance connects elevator availability with journey planning and assistance. No real station layout, current outage or operator detour has been copied into this example.',sources:['A02']},
    {heading:'Continue from the platform into the train',text:'Boarding aid needed retains a visible gap and stops the route at the platform. Aid in place adds a bridging plate and extends the trace through the doorway. Reaching the platform and being able to board are separate questions. RNIB’s public research summary identifies barriers involving ticketing, assistance, information, physical design and disruption. That evidence supports considering the whole journey, rather than treating a lift as a complete accessibility solution. It does not represent every passenger’s needs or validate the modeled route.',sources:['A03','A01']},
  ],sources:['A01','A02','A03']},
  {id:'configuration-handover',domain:'depot',view:'change-evidence',component:'change-item',title:'Match a changed assembly with its evidence',summary:'An installed module, a baseline, a test result and an acceptance record can describe different states.',sections:[
    {heading:'Identify the subject of each record',text:'The installed fictional module is version B with two connectors. In Installed, unmatched, the baseline and test still identify version A with a single mark. Baseline matched changes the configuration record, but the old test remains. The Crossrail public certification-package definition provides a concrete example of connecting design, construction, test, as-built, operational and maintenance information for an element. Its forms, identifiers and role allocation are not reproduced here.',sources:['Q01']},
    {heading:'Keep the test question specific',text:'The fictional requirement asks about reporting both input channels. Test recorded adds two channel traces and a B-subject test record, while the issue board remains. Reliability concerns failure behavior, availability concerns whether a function is available when needed, maintainability concerns the ability to maintain it, and safety concerns unacceptable risk. One functional check does not answer all four. In this example the outstanding item concerns access for maintenance; the other rows retain their separate question labels.',sources:['Q01','Q02']},
    {heading:'Read the handover decision and its limits',text:'Reviewed handover adds a decision record and an explicit disposition entry, while keeping the issue history. It is a fictional documentary comparison, not an approval mechanism. ORR’s public explanation concerns safety impact, significance and independent assessment in its Great Britain mainline context; the atlas neither makes those decisions nor generalizes them to every railway. Follow the connected maintenance-release and shared-dependency concepts to distinguish this evidence review from movement permission and service restoration.',sources:['Q02','Q01']},
  ],sources:['Q01','Q02']},
];

const tour=(id,n,title,domain,view,component,variant,explanation,actionText,question,choices,answer,feedback,sourceConcepts)=>({id,label:`${n} · ${title}`,title,domain,view,component,explanation,actionText,actionLabel:'Show this comparison',action:{view,component,variant,mode:'coasting',scenario:'nominal',exploded:0,cutaway:true},question,choices,answer,feedback,sourceConcepts});
